# Unwrapping Kaluza Klein

<Admonition title="Abstract" info>

The Kaluza–Klein model seeks to unify electromagnetism and gravity within a five-dimensional spacetime by introducing an additional spatial dimension whose geometry can encode electromagnetic and gravitational fields. In its traditional formulation, the fifth dimension is compactified, which was largely motivated by the fact that we don't appear to _observe_ a 4th spatial dimension.

Under the coordinate transformations proposed [here](https://flusterapp.com/blog/by_path/on_the_gravitational_nature_of_time), this 4th orthogonal spatial dimension becomes not only apparent, but directly measurable.

Here, we aim to bind this 4th spatial dimension with the unwrapped Kaluza-Klein geometry to compare the magnitudes between the coordinate transformations being proposed and the associated $\mathbf{KK}$ (Kaluza-Klein) dimension.

</Admonition>


## Interpreting The $\alpha \omega$ Geometry

Consider the Universe, where the 3 $\mathbb{R}^{3}$ axis' are compressed into a 2-dimensional plane. Time then acts as the orthogonal dimension, where each temporal 'slice' represents the state of the Universe in an instance of time. As 'time' progresses, an observer progresses along this orthogonal axis into a further dilated coordinate system. To an observer in motion along this axis, this appears to be the separation of coordinates as a function of time, giving rise to gravitational acceleration.

It's important to note here, that there is not necessarily any spatial separation between any two temporal slices, as the math appears to indicate that these fields directly overlap and are simply defined by the property that coordinate sets do not interact across some discrepancy in spatial density.

